Jim how would you recap what you saw  last night from start to finish  interesting you know coming in I think  we knew what to expect coming to a  strong military town you knew there was  going to be some backlash against Colin  but there also were a number of  supporters for Colin you know in the  stands a number of 49er fans what I  found most interesting is that in  talking to players and coaches from both  teams just how much support was really  there for him I mean I knew having  talked to some players prior to that but  once you got there and you were able to  look guys in the eye and really feel and  since you know their feelings about the  situation I was really struck by it and  and you know 149 player in particular  told me that if he had known Colin was  going to make this silent protest you  know initially that he would have taken  a knee with them or sat with him so I  think that's the thing that they're  outsiders and the noise wants to say  this is a distraction and what teams  going to put up with this and i can tell  you then across the league players  firmly support colin in terms of the  message he's trying to get out there  they might not necessarily agree with  the method but they definitely support  the message how did he perform I thought  he performed okay I mean the first drive  was important from the standpoint of  look he hasn't played good football for  several years now and in fact the last  few he's been regressing you could make  the case and he had a terrible preseason  game you know prior to this one and so  to go out in a hostile environment where  he knew so much attention was going to  be focused on him and to take them down  the field on the opening drive overcome  a couple of penalties and put them in  the end zone that was big now you know  you can say he was playing against  backups and we weren't game planning but  he also was playing with backups so from  that standpoint you can make the case  that the field was somewhat level but  you know it's going to be interesting to  me the next couple of days because  already I'm hearing this talk well now  the 49ers have to keep them the reality  is he wasn't going anywhere  based on this if Chip Kelly came out  earlier in the week and said he's one of  our two best quarterbacks so if the  49ers had released him by Saturday then  they would have given off the perception  that this was a political action instead  of a football decision and that's what  I'm saying to you I couldn't see any way  that he was going to be released going  into week one I still don't see it the  question I would want to ask him if I'm  Chip Kelly is what are you are you a  social activist moonlighting as a  quarterback or are you a quarterback  moonlighting as a social activist can  you do not I think Colin answered that  last night where he essentially said you  know they don't have to be mutually  exclusive he said I spent 12 hours a day  at the facility with my teammates with  my coaches and meetings and everything  else and once I leave their what I do on  my own time is my time so if you're  asking him I think he's going to tell  you he can do both and I find it  fascinating when people say well why now  as if there's some sort of timetable for  when young men have social awakenings  and if you talk to Colin again if you if  you look in his eyes and talk to people  around them there is the real sense that  this is very genuine and he is now he  has come into this at his own time you  know in terms of being socially aware  and wanting to address certain issues so  you know I firmly believe that this is  real that column believes that he can be  an effective quarterback and that he can  also try and and heighten the discussion  about something that he's very  passionate about it and feel serious  about yeah I tried to tell my audience  you know you may not like the message or  how the message is delivered I think  that's what bothers a lot of people but  I said listen to the message first and  we've moved away from what he said  initially and that's what bothered me I  may not like that he is kneeling but I'm  not walking in his shoes I I can only  try to understand what he is  representing or what he's seen or what  he's heard I can't sit here in my you  know lily white Palace and say man how  can you do that discrediting you know  the American flag I think if he had to  frame it different you know frame it  again  frame it a little bit different not  softer but different because I think it  came out that it was against the  military instead of it this is about the  police and that flag is not protecting  me the way it does white America if he  had a mulligan what'd he do it  differently you know I'm not so sure  that he would damn because I asked him  specifically last night I said how  disappointed are you that the further we  get away from people noticing your  initial protests that we've discussed  everything but the message behind your  protest yeah we've discussed your method  we've discussed the national anthem the  history of it the author of it we've  discussed whether or not we should even  have a national anthem and all of a  sudden this is morphed into Colin  doesn't respect the media when in fact  if you go back and you listen to his  initial press conference he never said  anything about the media in terms of  disrespecting it has always been on  point about police brutality you know  systemic police brutality and the  oppression of people of color so it's  funny that people then start to apply  their own beliefs so what the flag means  and everything else and you're right we  get further and further away from the  message and that's why I continue to ask  every day when the story comes up okay  we've talked about all these other  things but when are we actually going to  have a dialogue about his message and  Colin said last night he goes it's a  tough discussion to have and he believes  that's part of the reason that people  aren't talking about it and are talking  about all of these other things his Jim  Trotter covers the NFL for ESPN joining  a Stan Patrick show I mentioned last  hour that DeMarcus Cousins is doing  something and I said right but most of  you don't even know what he's doing and  he went to his home he's going to his  hometown of Mobile Alabama to get  together with the community and the  police defoors to unite and understand  to build trust but nobody's talking  about DeMarcus Cousins doing something  like that why correct because one I  think that again this is a tough  discussion to have and look it's always  easier in my opinion to find fault then  than to get behind something that may  make you a little bit  uncomfortable but it's the right thing  to do so from that standpoint the thing  you know last night Collin said he's  going to donate the first million  dollars of his salary to organizations  that are that are fighting for humanity  and for social justice now I asked them  specifically which organizations are you  talking to right now and he did want to  get into it at that point but the thing  that he said is he goes look I want to  go into communities I want to help I  want to be a part of the solution and  therefore you know I think with that  sort of call to arms by him I think  you're going to see more players I  grilling that yeah and getting out and I  think that I think it's fantastic so you  know ultimately I just I stand back  somewhat and I watch what has been the  reaction to him and how we get  distracted with all of these other  things and then I talked to these  athletes particularly ones last night  who tell me you know you can see this  matters to them because particularly  with the black athlete as they were  saying to me you know look we all know  someone who's been impacted by this if  it hasn't been us you know ourselves and  something needs to be done about it and  I think that the people are looking for  direction on 